We evaluate three formulations of execution-free verification---per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ)---across model sizes and complexity levels. Our experiments address five questions: (1)~How do \cls{} and \cwm{} compare at function level? (2)~Does the gap change with model size? (3)~Is per-test classification equivalent to trajectory-level scoring? (4)~Where does per-test granularity provide value? (5)~What error patterns emerge?

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We construct a function-level benchmark from HumanEval~\cite{chen2021humaneval} and MBPP~\cite{austin2021mbpp}. For each problem, we generate mutated variants using five mutation types: \texttt{wrong\_operator}, \texttt{off\_by\_one}, \texttt{boundary\_error}, \texttt{return\_value}, and \texttt{type\_error}. All labels are verified by executing each test with a 5-second timeout. The resulting dataset contains 19,076 samples (train: 15,290; validation: 1,896; test: 1,890) with a pass:fail ratio of approximately 52:48, making majority-baseline accuracy 51.75\%.

\paragraph{Models.}
We use Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B~\cite{yang2024qwen25} as base models. For zero-shot baselines, we evaluate Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-32B (4-bit NF4 quantization) as LLM-as-judge.

\paragraph{Training.}
All models are fine-tuned with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank=16, learning rate $2\times10^{-5}$, 3 epochs). We train with five random seeds (42, 123, 456, 789, 0). Seeds 42, 123, and 456 use full bfloat16 precision; seeds 789 and 0 use 4-bit NF4 quantization~\cite{dettmers2024qlora} due to GPU memory constraints. Where precision matters for cross-formulation comparison, we report matched-precision subsets explicitly.

\paragraph{Formulations.}
\textbf{\cls{}}: given a code context and test input, predict a single token \texttt{PASS} or \texttt{FAIL}; trained with cross-entropy loss.
\textbf{\cwm{}}: given a code context and test input, generate the execution output string; verdict is derived by exact match against expected output.
\textbf{TRAJ}: given a code context and the full test suite, predict \texttt{RESOLVED} or \texttt{UNRESOLVED} at the patch level; provides no per-test signal.

\paragraph{Oracle analysis.}
We use the SWE-bench Verified split~\cite{chowdhury2024swebenchverified} (500 instances) to characterize the distribution of FAIL\_TO\_PASS (F2P) and PASS\_TO\_PASS (P2P) tests and to derive the accuracy-utility frontier (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\paragraph{Statistical tests.}
We use clustered bootstrap~\cite{davison1997bootstrap} (114 problem-level clusters, 10{,}000 resamples) for confidence intervals and $p$-values, and the Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) procedure~\cite{schuirmann1987tost} with a $\pm$2\pp{} equivalence margin for the per-test vs.\ trajectory-level comparison.

