\subsection{CWM Failure Modes}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

The generation formulation introduces two systematic failure modes absent in classification:

\paragraph{Token-budget exhaustion.} When the expected output $o_i^*$ exceeds the model's generation budget $B$ (i.e., $|o_i^*| > B$), the predicted output $\hat{o}_i$ is necessarily truncated, guaranteeing exact-match failure regardless of prediction quality. This affects tests involving long outputs---complex data structures, multi-line traces, or iterative computations---which become systematically unverifiable under \textsc{CWM}.

\paragraph{String-value prediction errors.} Even within the token budget, exact match requires character-level fidelity. Semantically equivalent outputs that differ in surface form---floating-point precision, whitespace conventions, dictionary ordering---produce false negatives. The probability of such errors grows with output complexity and the number of independently predicted tokens: each additional token introduces a multiplicative chance of mismatch.

\textsc{CLS} bypasses both failure modes by construction: its single-token output cannot be truncated and requires no string matching. We hypothesize that this asymmetry produces a \emph{complexity-dependent advantage}: on simple code with short execution outputs, both formulations perform comparably because CWM failure modes rarely trigger; on complex code with long and structured outputs, the advantage of \textsc{CLS} grows as failure modes increasingly affect \textsc{CWM}.

