\subsection{CWM Failure Modes}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

The generation formulation introduces two systematic failure modes absent in classification. \emph{Token-budget exhaustion}: when $|o_i^*| > B$ (generation budget), the output is truncated, guaranteeing exact-match failure regardless of prediction quality. \emph{String-value prediction errors}: even within budget, semantically equivalent outputs differing in surface form (floating-point precision, dictionary ordering, whitespace) produce false negatives, with error probability growing multiplicatively in output length.

\textsc{CLS} bypasses both by construction: its single-token output cannot be truncated and requires no string matching. We hypothesize a \emph{complexity-dependent advantage}: both formulations perform comparably on simple code with short outputs, but \textsc{CLS} gains as output complexity triggers CWM failure modes.

